the system is second order and energy is monitored: symplectic integrators keep energy error bounded over long times instead of letting it drift
at tolerance 1.00e-9 a fourth-order composition (Yoshida) needs far fewer steps than second-order velocity Verlet, so Yoshida's symplectic composition (order 4) was chosen
the step comes from rtol = 1.00e-9 and atol = 1.00e-12: a probe (adaptive step doubling, where one step compared with two half steps estimates the local error) ran over the whole interval in 450 steps and estimated the largest step the tolerance allows at each point; the smallest was 0.00756 s, and 0.8 times that, 0.00605 s, is held fixed for the whole run, because varying the step would forfeit the bounded-energy property
but a trial run at that step over the whole interval showed energy drifting by 2.60e-9 (relative), more than the 1.00e-9 the tolerance asks, so the step was reduced to 0.00429 s (a fourth-order method's bounded error scales like the step to that power), where the trial drift was 6.56e-10
took 1477 fixed steps of at most 0.00429 s
energy drifted by at most 6.5e-10 (relative)
